From: Prabhsimran Singh <prabhsimrans@nvidia.com>
Subject: llama: DFlash2 drafting in the batched serving path

DFlash2 drafts a block of tokens per sequence from the target's hidden features. In the batched serving path (both
contexts with llama_serving_params.batched) a drafting step no longer round-trips through the host:

- every target decode writes the residual stream at the inputs of the configured layers into one device tensor
  (llama_set_layer_features_device; BF16 [n_layers*n_embd, n_batch], row = batch index), which the drafter's injection
  pass reads in place; GPU events (llama_record_event) order the two contexts, and the target's next pass waits for
  the injection on the GPU;
- the drafter decodes every drafting sequence's block [last id, n_draft masks] in one ubatch with a non-causal mask
  (a query sees its whole sequence up to the block's last position);
- the drafts are walked on the device: the target's LM head on the n_draft rows after each block's anchor in row
  chunks, top-k candidates (DFLASH_CONV and the small-k top-k from the previous patches), the selector's edge scores
  and the greedy left-to-right walk; llama_get_draft_ids returns n_draft ids per sequence after one wait.

Rejected verify rows are removed by the caller (llama_memory_seq_rm), so they never reach a draft.

Upstream: not planned in this form (batched serving path).

